const workshopCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'm', label: 'mass m', min: 1, max: 100, step: 1, value: 60, unit: 'kg' },
    { name: 'V', label: 'volume V', min: 1000, max: 100000, step: 500, value: 60000, unit: 'cm³' },
    { name: 'h', label: 'height climbed h', min: 0.5, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 3, unit: 'm' },
    { name: 't', label: 'time taken t', min: 1, max: 30, step: 0.5, value: 5, unit: 's' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'density ρ = m/V', expr: '1000000 * m / V', unit: 'kg/m³' },
    { label: 'energy of the climb E = m·g·h', expr: 'm * 9.81 * h', unit: 'J' },
    { label: 'useful power P = E/t', expr: 'm * 9.81 * h / t', unit: 'W' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/foundational/energy-conservation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Energy is the universe's accounting system. Every physical process — a cart rolling down a
    hill, a battery draining, your breakfast being digested — is a <em>transfer</em> of energy
    from one store to another, and the books never fail to balance. That is the
    <strong>conservation of energy</strong>: energy cannot be created or destroyed, only moved
    between stores or spread out. It is the single most trustworthy statement in science, trusted
    precisely because every attempt to catch it cheating — from Victorian perpetual-motion
    machines to modern cold-fusion claims — has failed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two stores matter most for moving objects. A moving object carries <strong>kinetic
    energy</strong>, <code>KE = ½·m·v²</code>; an elevated object carries <strong>gravitational
    potential energy</strong>, <code>GPE = m·g·h</code>. On an ideal frictionless track the two
    trade back and forth, but their sum never changes. The cart below starts at height h₀ from
    rest, so its speed at any point is fixed by how much height it has lost.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two hills, one budget — lower the start height and watch the cart fail the first hill" widget="plotter" config={coasterCfg} />

  <p>
    Try h₀ ≈ 6: the speed curve snaps where the track rises above the start height. That break is
    the conservation law speaking — no energy, no climb. Now watch the reverse: at every valley the
    speed peaks exactly where the height bottoms out. Roller-coaster designers live inside this
    diagram, which is why the first hill is always the tallest and every later hill must be
    shorter: friction leaks a little of the budget with every metre of track, and there is no
    overdraft facility.
  </p>

  <h2>Density: how tightly matter is packed</h2>
  <p>
    Before energy, one measurement deserves its own section because it decides whether things
    float, sink or fly. <strong>Density</strong> is mass per unit volume: <code>ρ = m / V</code>,
    measured in kg/m³ (or g/cm³, where 1 g/cm³ = 1,000 kg/m³). Water is 1.0 g/cm³, air about
    0.0012 g/cm³, lead 11.3 g/cm³. Density is a property of the <em>material</em>, not the object:
    a gram of lead and a tonne of lead have the same density, while a tonne of feathers and a
    gram of feathers also match each other — this is the resolution of the classic riddle.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example: a metal block has mass 120 g and dimensions 4 cm × 3 cm × 2.5 cm. Its volume
    is 4 × 3 × 2.5 = 30 cm³, so its density is 120 ÷ 30 = 4.0 g/cm³ — plausible for a titanium
    alloy. To measure the volume of an irregular object, immerse it: the rise in water level (or
    the overflow from a full displacement can) equals the object's volume, a technique
    Archimedes famously used to test whether a king's crown was pure gold. To measure the density
    of a liquid, weigh a known volume using a measuring cylinder on a balance and divide.
  </p>
  <p>
    Density decides floating. An object placed in a fluid floats if its average density is less
    than the fluid's, because the fluid can supply an upward force equal to the weight of
    displaced fluid — and if the object is less dense than the fluid, that buoyant force reaches
    the object's full weight while the object is still only partly submerged. Ice (0.92 g/cm³)
    floats on water with about 92% of it hidden below the surface — which is why icebergs are so
    deceptive and why lakes freeze from the top down, insulating the fish below.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "heavy things sink." A 100,000-tonne ship floats happily
  because its average density — steel hull <em>plus</em> all the air inside — is well below
  water's. A tiny pebble sinks because its density is above water's. It is always density
  compared with the fluid, never weight alone.</p>

  <h2>Energy stores and how they are named</h2>
  <p>
    Physics sorts energy into <em>stores</em>: kinetic (motion), gravitational potential (height),
    elastic (stretched springs, bent bows), chemical (food, fuel, batteries), nuclear (nuclei),
    and thermal (the random jiggling of particles). Transfers between them happen by mechanical
    working, electrical working, heating, or radiation. A ball thrown upward is a three-act play:
    kinetic drains into gravitational potential on the way up, and back again on the way down. A
    burning match moves energy from the chemical store to thermal and to radiation. Naming the
    stores keeps the accounting honest — and it exposes the crucial fact that
    <strong>energy is a scalar</strong>. Unlike force, energy has no direction: you add energies
    from every source with plain arithmetic, which is why conservation is such a clean law.
  </p>
  <p>
    The unit is the joule (J): lifting an apple (≈100 g) one metre takes about 1 J. A few
    benchmarks to calibrate your intuition: a smartphone charge ≈ 10,000 J, a chocolate bar ≈
    1,000,000 J, a lightning bolt ≈ 1,000,000,000 J, and a house consumes roughly 30 J every
    second just keeping the fridge cold.
  </p>

  <h2>Work: paying for energy in force × distance</h2>
  <p>
    When energy is transferred by a force acting along a distance, physicists call the payment
    <strong>work</strong>: <code>W = F·d</code>, force times the distance moved <em>in the
    direction of the force</em>, measured in joules (1 J = 1 N·m). Lifting a 2 kg box 1.5 m at
    steady speed needs an upward force equal to its weight, 2 × 9.8 = 19.6 N, so the work done is
    19.6 × 1.5 ≈ 29 J — and sure enough, the box has gained 29 J of gravitational potential
    energy (m·g·h again). The two formulas are the same accounting viewed from different sides.
  </p>
  <p>
    The "in the direction of the force" clause is where marks are won and lost. Carrying the box
    horizontally does <em>no</em> work against gravity, because the motion is perpendicular to
    the weight. And merely <em>holding</em> the box does no work at all — there is force but no
    distance, which is why standing still holding luggage is exhausting but does not warm the
    luggage. Friction, meanwhile, always charges for distance: drag it 3 m with a 50 N horizontal
    force and you pay 150 J, most of which friction promptly converts to thermal energy in the
    floor.
  </p>

  <h2>Power: the rate of the payment</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Power</strong> is energy transferred per second: <code>P = E / t</code>, measured in
    watts (1 W = 1 J/s). Energy tells you <em>how much</em>; power tells you <em>how fast</em>.
    Running up a flight of stairs: a 60 kg student climbing 3 m gains 60 × 9.8 × 3 ≈ 1,760 J of
    potential energy. Sprint it in 5 s and the useful power is 1,760 ÷ 5 ≈ 350 W — about half a
    horse's worth, briefly. Take the stairs gently in 15 s and the same 1,760 J transfers at only
    117 W. Same energy, different power: the two words are not interchangeable, and confusing
    them is the most common error in everyday energy talk ("my electricity bill is measured in
    kilowatt-hours" — that is energy, because the kilowatt is multiplied by hours).
  </p>
  <p>
    Household scale: an electric kettle draws about 2,000 W and boils a litre in a couple of
    minutes; a typical car engine delivers up to 100,000 W (100 kW) flat out. Bills are actually
    in kilowatt-hours, where 1 kWh = 3,600,000 J — the energy of a 1 kW appliance running for an
    hour. All the formulas on this page are collected in the panel below: set the mass and volume
    of an object, watch its density; then set a climb and a time, and read off both the energy
    bill and the power.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="three formulas, one dial panel — density, energy of a climb, and the power it takes" widget="sliderLab" config={workshopCfg} />

  <h2>Energy resources: where civilisation draws its power</h2>
  <p>
    Every power station, panel and turbine on Earth is rearranging the same energy budget.
    <strong>Non-renewable</strong> resources draw down stores that took geologic ages to fill:
    fossil fuels (chemical store, from ancient sunlight) and uranium (nuclear store).
    <strong>Renewables</strong> tap flows that keep arriving: solar radiation, wind (the Sun's
    heating of the atmosphere, kinetic store), hydroelectric (gravitational potential of rain
    lifted by evaporation), biomass, geothermal heat, and tides (gravitational, from the Moon's
    pull). Each choice trades off energy density, reliability, cost and environmental damage — a
    coal train vs a uranium flask vs an offshore wind farm are engineering decisions as much as
    physics ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    No conversion is perfect, and <strong>efficiency</strong> measures the shortfall:
    <code>efficiency = useful energy out ÷ total energy in</code>, often ×100%. An old
    incandescent bulb converts about 5% of its electrical input into light — the rest heats the
    room (occasionally welcome, usually not); an LED manages above 50%. A typical thermal power
    station converts roughly 35–40% of its fuel's energy into electricity, dumping the rest into
    rivers or the air as low-grade heat, a fundamental limit imposed by thermodynamics rather
    than poor engineering. The first law guarantees the missing energy still exists; the sad
    second-law truth is that it is no longer useful.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> energy is not "used up" — it is <em>degraded</em>. After your
  car journey, every joule that started in the petrol still exists, but now it is a spread-out
  warm haze of exhaust heat and road friction, too diffuse to push anything. "Energy crisis" is
  really a <em>useful-energy</em> crisis.</p>

  <h2>Pressure: force spread over area</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Pressure</strong> is force concentrated or spread: <code>p = F / A</code>, force
    divided by the area it acts over, measured in pascals (1 Pa = 1 N/m²). The same force can be
    devastating or harmless depending on the area. A drawing pin: your thumb supplies maybe 30 N,
    but concentrated on a tip a fraction of a millimetre across it exerts tens of millions of
    pascals — enough to pierce wood — while the broad flat head spreads the same 30 N so your
    thumb feels nothing sharp. Skis and snowshoes spread a person's weight over a large area to
    walk on snow; stiletto heels do the opposite and dent wooden floors. A 60 kg person standing
    on both feet (weight 588 N over a contact area of roughly 0.04 m²) presses at about 15 kPa;
    on the few square millimetres of a heel, pressures reach megapascals.
  </p>
  <p>
    In fluids, pressure grows with depth because the weight of fluid above stacks up:
    <code>p = ρ·g·h</code>. Dive 10 m underwater and you add about 1000 × 9.8 × 10 ≈ 98,000 Pa —
    roughly one extra atmosphere — which is why ears pop and why submarines are engineered like
    eggs: strong against outside pressure, weak against inside. Fluid pressure acts in all
    directions and transmits unchanged through a connected fluid, which is the whole basis of
    hydraulics: a small force on a small piston becomes a large force on a large piston, at the
    cost of pushing the small piston much further — force multiplied, work conserved, energy's
    accounting intact once again.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Where does <code>KE = ½·m·v²</code> come from? Not from thin air — from the work definition
    plus the equations of motion. Accelerate a mass m from rest with force F over distance s:
    the work done is F·s. From <code>v² = u² + 2·a·s</code> with u = 0, s = v²/(2a), so
    F·s = (m·a)·(v²/2a) = ½·m·v². The ½ is not arbitrary; it is the fingerprint of distance
    growing as the <em>square</em> of time during acceleration. This is also exactly why braking
    distance scales with the square of speed — a fact the motion lesson proved independently,
    now re-derived from an energy point of view. Two different roads to the same formula is
    what physicists mean when they say a result is "solid".
  </p>
  <p>
    The energy idea reaches further than the syllabus suggests. Chemical energy turns out to be
    electrical potential energy between atoms; thermal energy is kinetic energy of
    sub-microscopic jiggling; mass itself is a store — Einstein's <code>E = m·c²</code> means
    1 kg of matter is worth about 9 × 10¹⁶ J, and the Sun converts roughly 4 million tonnes of
    mass to radiated energy every second. At the frontier, cosmology's biggest open problem is a
    form of energy: "dark energy", making up about 68% of the universe, whose nature nobody
    knows. The accounting system runs on scales from a drawing pin to the cosmos — with, so far,
    no exception ever found.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, <em>College Physics</em> (Work and Kinetic Energy; Fluid Mechanics); Feynman, <em>Lectures on Physics</em>, Vol. I; Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus (2026–2028), sections 1.4, 1.7 and 1.8; IEA, Key World Energy Statistics.</p>
</TopicPage>
